# Shape of <overlay>/config/deployment.env — facts that are true of the whole
# deployment rather than of one capability or one machine. Copy it into the active
# overlay's config/ directory and fill it in.
#
# Plain KEY=value, read by libs/axon-config (deployment_home_timezone) and
# libs/axon-server (the inbound token reference). axon-config is deliberately
# dependency-free, so this file is not JSON or TOML: adding a parser to read one string
# would change the dependency resolution of every capability that consumes it.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. A value that several capabilities need has to be declared
# once. Before this, calendar and scouting each carried their own home_timezone, which
# is two values that agree right up until one of them is edited — and a wrong zone
# does not fail, it silently writes every stored wall time an hour or more off.
#
# NOT FOR SECRETS. This is a plain tracked-shape file in the overlay; real secret
# values stay in Vaultwarden and reach a capability through its own env_file (see
# schemas/service.toml.example and tools/setup-secret.sh). A PATH to a secret is not
# a secret, which is why SJEL_INBOUND_TOKEN_FILE below is a reference and never a
# value — the same split comms already made with api_secret_file.
#
# NOT FOR MACHINE FACTS. os, container_runtime, the enabled capability set and the
# state-mount registry describe one machine, not the deployment — those live in
# <overlay>/config/machine.toml (schemas/machine.toml.example).

# The one zone every stored wall time is in. No default on purpose: capabilities
# refuse to guess rather than writing a silently wrong hour. Accepts UTC, a fixed
# ±HH:MM offset, or one of the supported IANA zones listed in each consumer
# (capabilities/calendar/src/zone.rs, capabilities/scouting/src/localtime.rs).
#
# A capability-level "home_timezone" still overrides this, so an existing config keeps
# working. What it may not do is disagree: two different values are an error naming
# both files, not a precedence rule, because drift is the failure this prevents.
SJEL_HOME_TIMEZONE=Europe/Berlin

# The shared secret that admits an inbound HTTP request to any capability server on
# this deployment, given as the PATH of a private file holding the token — never the
# token itself. Read by libs/axon-server; see libs/axon-server/README.md for the full
# contract. Absent by default, and absent is a working deployment: every capability
# server then keeps the loopback-only, unauthenticated shape it has always had.
#
# Set it and two things change together, which is the point:
#   - every route except /health and /ready asks for the token, on every capability
#     that starts through axon_server::serve_local (eleven of twelve today);
#   - a server may bind beyond 127.0.0.1 at all. Without a token that bind is
#     refused, so "reachable from the phone" cannot arrive before the gate does.
#
# One token for the deployment, not one per capability: it gates one thing — whether
# a request reached this machine legitimately — and per-capability tokens would be
# one secret per port for every client that fans out across them (the dashboard's
# Vite proxy, axon-status' /routes aggregation).
#
# The file it points at holds either the bare token or JSON with .auth.api_key.
# Generate one privately, never through an agent and never into a tracked file:
#   openssl rand -hex -out <path> 32 && chmod 600 <path>
#
# comms' own api_secret_file still wins for comms, because the browser extension,
# axon-clip and the dashboard proxy already hold that value. Point both references at
# the same file to converge on one token; until then axon-status cannot read comms'
# /routes and reports it as unavailable.
# SJEL_INBOUND_TOKEN_FILE=/absolute/path/to/inbound-token

# The tailnet login admitted to every capability server on this deployment, as a
# VALUE rather than a file reference — unlike the token above. A login is not a
# credential: it appears in the tailnet admin console, in `tailscale status`, and
# in the header of every request that arrives. The split this file documents is
# exactly the one between those two things.
#
# WHY A SECOND GATE. The token cannot reach the caller the tailnet exists to
# serve. A browser on the phone loads the built SPA and issues relative fetches;
# giving that page the token means shipping the deployment's shared secret into a
# bundle and every cache that touches it. `tailscale serve` already knows who the
# caller is, so the gate reads that instead (libs/axon-server/src/tailnet.rs).
#
# WHAT MAKES THE HEADER TRUSTWORTHY. The proxy overwrites it. Measured against
# tailscale 1.102.3 on 2026-09-06: a request carrying
# `Tailscale-User-Login: attacker@evil.example` reached the backend as the
# authenticated node's own login. A client cannot inject an identity through the
# proxy — it can only fail to have one.
#
# Set it and, on every capability that starts through axon_server::serve_local:
#   - a request the proxy proved came from this login is served, with no token;
#   - a request the proxy proved came from anyone else is 401, token or not;
#   - a request with no identity header falls through to the token rule above,
#     unchanged. That is a direct loopback caller — or a proxy that stopped
#     injecting identity, which is why `tools/doctor` checks the serve shape.
#
# It never satisfies a capability's refuse_without_token: comms turns that on
# because POST /ingest fetches an attacker-chosen URL, and a route with that
# property wants the secret, not a name.
#
# Absent by default, and absent is a working deployment: the identity header is
# then ignored entirely rather than believed, because declaring nothing must not
# silently start trusting a header any caller can write.
# SJEL_TAILNET_OPERATOR=you@example.com

# ── The local-network listener (PRD Q119) ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# A second port on every interface where a paired device reaches Axon with no
# tailnet: TLS with a self-signed certificate the phone pins, and a gate that
# admits device-signed requests only (plus the one-time pairing claim). The
# certificate and its key are made on first start under <overlay>/data/lan/.
# The shell advertises it with Bonjour as _axon._tcp.
#
# Absent by default: no LAN port is opened.
# SJEL_LAN_PORT=8443
